# Flutter Security Rules

## 1. Secrets

Never commit:

- API keys
- passwords
- private keys
- tokens
- production credentials
- client secrets

## 2. Configuration

Environment-specific configuration must not be hardcoded into application logic.

## 3. Authentication Tokens

Persist sensitive authentication material using secure platform storage.

Do not store authentication tokens in plain text storage unless explicitly justified by the security architecture.

## 4. Authorization

Flutter must never be treated as the authority for authorization.

UI restrictions are not security controls.

The backend must authorize every protected operation.

## 5. Tenant Isolation

The client must never allow the user to arbitrarily select or manipulate tenant identifiers unless explicitly authorized by the backend contract.

Never trust tenant identifiers received from UI input.

## 6. Logging

Never log:

- passwords
- authentication tokens
- secrets
- sensitive personal information

Production logging must be intentionally controlled.

## 7. Error Messages

Do not expose:

- stack traces
- database errors
- internal service details
- credentials
- infrastructure details

to end users.

## 8. Web Views

Do not embed authentication or sensitive workflows into arbitrary web views without explicit security justification.

## 9. Dependencies

Security-sensitive packages must be kept updated.

Do not introduce packages with unclear maintenance or security posture.